    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0097",
      "sequence": 109,
      "meaning": "Mainā asks what wrong she ever did Nārada, who ruined her settled home by instructing Umā to perform austerity for a mad groom. Truly he has neither attachment nor family: detached, without wealth, house or wife, a destroyer of others’ homes, he has no shame or fear—how could a barren woman know childbirth’s pain? Seeing her mother distraught, Bhavānī speaks gently and discerningly: she should recognize fate and not grieve, for what the Creator ordains cannot be averted. If destiny has written a mad husband, why blame anyone? Can Mainā erase fate’s inscriptions? She should not take needless disgrace upon herself.",
